       ; iterating remembered_set\nVM::collect_garbage + 5444\nfinish_alloc + 424\nstring::plus + 400\ndispatch_method + 404\nwren_num_add + 676\nwlift_aot_mod_1__method_6_20 + 2264\n```\n\nThe remembered_set entry being traced is a dangling pointer.\nremembered_set is populated by `write_barrier(source, value)`.\nThe source is supposed to be a live old-gen object — but here\nit's stale, indicating AOT-emitted code passed a freed pointer\nto `wren_write_barrier`. The freed pointer is an old-gen object\nthat got swept by a previous major GC; the AOT code held the\npointer in a register/spill across that GC and the spill slot\nisn't covered by `set_jit_roots` / `JIT_ROOTS_STORE`.\n\nThis is the deeper root-coverage issue, not a `ptr::read`\naliasing one. Stack maps would also fix it (if the spill is in\nthe stack map, the GC would have marked the object live during\nmark phase and it wouldn't have been swept). The remaining\ncoverage gaps live in:\n\n1. Stale-pointer sources in `wren_write_barrier` calls — every\n   AOT-emitted store to a heap object's field calls\n   `wren_write_barrier(source, value)`. The source needs to be\n   in stack maps right up to the barrier call.\n2. SM cross-fn invocation's `fiber` argument — held across\n   many helper calls; partially covered, but inspection paths\n   weren't all instrumented.\n3. CallNamed / dispatch paths for fast/slow tier fallbacks —\n   intermediate Values that survive across the fast→slow\n   transition.\n\n\n- Audit every `wren_write_barrier` invocation site in\n  `cranelift_backend.rs` lowerings. Confirm the source Value\n  is in stack maps right at the call. Especially in subscript\n  set / instance field write / map set paths.\n- Validate by running the site with workaround off + an\n  instrumented `wren_write_barrier` that asserts\n  `source` is in `old_objects` linked list before pushing to\n  remembered_set. If the assert fires, the offending AOT\n  function is named in the crash report.\n- After that, the next likely class is closure-upvalue\n  capture (`MakeClosure` with \u003e8 upvalues stores values to a\n  stack slot; the slot itself isn't covered by stack maps).\n\n\nThe workaround at c570b9a forces every alloc through\n`alloc_old`. Effects:\n- No `ptr::read` promotion → can't have promotion-aliasing.\n- Objects don't move → AOT code's raw pointers stay valid\n  across GC for the object's lifetime.\n- sweep_old still frees objects → AOT code's pointer to a\n  swept object is still dangling. **This is still happening**\n  even with the workaround, but the GC's iteration order may\n  not hit the dangling pointer on the hot path.\n\nRemoving the workaround requires the stack-map work above plus\nensuring every AOT-held Value is rooted across every safepoint\nthat could trigger sweep. Multi-week.","files":null}]}